{"availability":"available","full":"Vanshbordia/imagetools","name":"imagetools","description":"Explore this public GitHub project.","category":"Developer tools","language":"TypeScript","stars":1,"forks":0,"created":"2024-09-06T22:29:47Z","updated":"2026-09-14T13:54:10Z","license":"MIT","branch":"main","homepage":"https://imagetools-57v.pages.dev/","topics":["compress-images","image-tools"],"demo":"https://imagetools-57v.pages.dev/","color":"#302e55","ink":"#dbd2ff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-19T00:43:25.583Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-19T00:43:25.583Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-19T00:43:25.583Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-09T15:38:16.447Z","stars":1,"forks":0},{"at":"2026-10-12T23:50:03.757Z","stars":1,"forks":0},{"at":"2026-10-19T00:43:25.583Z","stars":1,"forks":0}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["A fast, no-nonsense image converter that runs entirely in your browser.","I needed WebP images at specific dimensions and quality levels, on a per-image basis. Writing a shell script felt wrong because every image needed slightly different treatment. Photoshop takes forever to boot and is overkill for this. Canva and similar tools are either too restrictive or put things behind a paywall. I just wanted something that opens instantly, lets me see what I'm doing, and gets out of the way.","So I built this. Everything runs in the browser via libvips compiled to WebAssembly. No backend, no uploads, no waiting for a server. Drop an image, pick a format and quality, convert, download. Done."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/Vanshbordia/imagetools/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"392849f28b77d699b94ebded91646fc087f38fa1cfdb4b13e2baad3cbe8356da","extractedAt":"2026-10-09T15:38:16.605Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-09T15:38:16.605Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"A fast, no-nonsense image converter that runs entirely in your browser.","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/Vanshbordia/imagetools/blob/main/READM